    "['mpls ldp', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ldp interface all', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set protocols ldp interface all', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols ldp interface all\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, if you want to be more specific about the interfaces (similar to the Huawei config where LDP is enabled on specific interfaces), you could use:\\n##\\nset protocols ldp interface ge-1/0/1.0\\nset protocols ldp interface ge-1/0/3.0\\n##\\n\\nNote: The Juniper equivalent enables LDP globally and then specifies interfaces, whereas Huawei enables it per-interface. Both approaches achieve the same result. The first option is simpler but enables LDP on all interfaces, while the second matches Huawei's interface-specific approach more closely.\", 'trans_template': None}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ldp interface ge-1/0/1.0', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set protocols ldp interface ge-1/0/1.0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols ldp interface all\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, if you want to be more specific about the interfaces (similar to the Huawei config where LDP is enabled on specific interfaces), you could use:\\n##\\nset protocols ldp interface ge-1/0/1.0\\nset protocols ldp interface ge-1/0/3.0\\n##\\n\\nNote: The Juniper equivalent enables LDP globally and then specifies interfaces, whereas Huawei enables it per-interface. Both approaches achieve the same result. The first option is simpler but enables LDP on all interfaces, while the second matches Huawei's interface-specific approach more closely.\", 'trans_template': None}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ldp interface ge-1/0/3.0', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set protocols ldp interface ge-1/0/3.0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols ldp interface all\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, if you want to be more specific about the interfaces (similar to the Huawei config where LDP is enabled on specific interfaces), you could use:\\n##\\nset protocols ldp interface ge-1/0/1.0\\nset protocols ldp interface ge-1/0/3.0\\n##\\n\\nNote: The Juniper equivalent enables LDP globally and then specifies interfaces, whereas Huawei enables it per-interface. Both approaches achieve the same result. The first option is simpler but enables LDP on all interfaces, while the second matches Huawei's interface-specific approach more closely.\", 'trans_template': None}]]": 1,
    "['mpls', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols mpls interface all', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set protocols mpls interface all', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols mpls interface all\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, MPLS is enabled globally under `protocols mpls`.\\n- The `interface all` statement enables MPLS on all interfaces (similar to Huawei's global `mpls` command without interface specification). \\n- For more precise interface control in Juniper, you'd specify interfaces individually (e.g., `set protocols mpls interface ge-0/0/0.0`).\", 'trans_template': None}]]": 1,
